<?xml version="1.0"?>
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">
<!--
  6-DOF arm with a SPHERICAL WRIST.

    J1 yaw (z) == riser == J2 pitch (y) == L1 == J3 elbow pitch (y) == L2
       == J4 roll (x) == J5 pitch (y) == J6 roll (x) == palm + 2 fingers

  The J4/J5/J6 axes all intersect at the wrist centre, which is what keeps the
  inverse kinematics closed form: solve the wrist centre from J1..J3, then
  extract J4..J6 from the residual rotation as X-Y-X Euler angles.

  WHY SIX. A 4-DOF arm can put the hand anywhere but can only orient it within
  one vertical plane, and that plane is fixed by where the hand has to be. Two
  hands holding an object's two ends through a 90 deg flip need each hand's FULL
  orientation to follow the object. Sampling that motion showed the wrist pitch
  would have to reach 128 deg while the approach direction was simultaneously
  constrained - impossible with fewer than three wrist axes. With three, and a
  wrist pitch range of +/-140 deg, all 36 tested configurations solve.

  Conventions (must match the behaviour package's ik.py):
   * positive J2/J3/J5 pitch rotates DOWN (right-hand rule about +y)
   * the grasp point is LG = 0.07 m beyond the wrist centre along the tool x axis
   * fingers are prismatic and close along the palm's local y; positive = OPEN
-->

  <!-- where the suction cup sits along the finger: the object's equator -->
  <xacro:property name="RIDGE_X_CUP" value="0.065"/>

  <xacro:macro name="arm_6dof" params="side reflect finger_effort:=6.0">

    <!-- === J1 yaw + riser === -->
    <link name="${side}_shoulder_link">
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="0 0 0.05"/>
        <geometry><cylinder radius="0.020" length="0.10"/></geometry>
        <material name="arm_blue"/>
      </visual>
      <collision>
        <origin xyz="0 0 0.05"/>
        <geometry><cylinder radius="0.020" length="0.10"/></geometry>
      </collision>
      <xacro:cyl_inertial m="0.16" r="0.020" h="0.10">
        <origin xyz="0 0 0.05"/>
      </xacro:cyl_inertial>
    </link>
    <joint name="${side}_j1" type="revolute">
      <parent link="base_link"/>
      <child link="${side}_shoulder_link"/>
      <origin xyz="${arm_mount_x} ${reflect*arm_mount_y} ${deck_top_z}"/>
      <axis xyz="0 0 1"/>
      <!-- EFFORT 12 N.m, was 6.0. The shoulder pitch needs 2.17 N.m to hold the arm
           static and 3.74 N.m with a 0.5 kg payload (computed in the companion
           rigid-body dynamics library), so a 6 N.m limit left only 1.6x margin before
           the joint saturates - and a saturated joint cannot reach its commanded
           position, which is what "arms did not converge, left_j5 off 0.642" looks
           like. The errors were worse LOADED than unloaded, which is the signature.
           12 N.m is about 3x the loaded static requirement, leaving room for the
           acceleration of the move itself. This is a limit, not a command: it does not
           make the arm push harder, only stops it being clipped. -->
      <limit lower="${-yaw_lim}" upper="${yaw_lim}" effort="12.0" velocity="3.0"/>
      <dynamics damping="0.7" friction="0.05"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- === J2 shoulder pitch + the pitch yoke === -->
    <!-- The yoke exists only to give J2b a child. It is the housing the roll actuator
         turns inside, so it carries that actuator's mass: 0.12 kg, ON the roll axis, so
         it adds essentially nothing to the shoulder pitch torque (lever arm ~0) while
         still being counted in the 5 kg Kobuki budget. See MASS_BUDGET.md. -->
    <link name="${side}_upper_yoke_link">
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="${PI/2} 0 0"/>
        <geometry><cylinder radius="0.022" length="0.05"/></geometry>
        <material name="dark_grey"/>
      </visual>
      <collision>
        <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="${PI/2} 0 0"/>
        <geometry><cylinder radius="0.022" length="0.05"/></geometry>
      </collision>
      <xacro:box_inertial m="0.12" x="0.044" y="0.05" z="0.044">
        <origin xyz="0 0 0"/>
      </xacro:box_inertial>
    </link>
    <joint name="${side}_j2" type="revolute">
      <parent link="${side}_shoulder_link"/>
      <child link="${side}_upper_yoke_link"/>
      <origin xyz="0 0 ${shoulder_z - deck_top_z}"/>
      <axis xyz="0 1 0"/>
      <limit lower="${-pitch2_lim}" upper="${pitch2_lim}" effort="12.0" velocity="3.0"/>
      <dynamics damping="0.7" friction="0.05"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- === J2b SHOULDER ROLL - THE SEVENTH AXIS === -->
    <!-- Rolls about the upper arm's OWN axis, so it does not move the elbow: the elbow
         sits L1 along local x whatever the roll is. What it moves is the PLANE the
         forearm swings in, which is the elbow-swivel redundancy a 6-DOF arm lacks.
         Six axes reach the swivel circle at only two points - elbow up and elbow down -
         and in this task elbow-up collides arm-to-arm at the coaxial 90 deg pose while
         elbow-down enters the base on the descent, with a 207 deg jump between them.
         Measured with the box-decomposed collision model, 6 DOF also puts the finger
         inside its own forearm on 23 of 94 arm-poses, worst -19.6 mm.
         Placed at the shoulder because that is the one spot where added mass loads
         nothing downstream. -->
    <link name="${side}_upper_arm_link">
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="${L1/2} 0 0"/>
        <geometry><box size="${L1} 0.045 0.032"/></geometry>
        <material name="arm_blue"/>
      </visual>
      <collision>
        <origin xyz="${L1/2} 0 0"/>
        <geometry><box size="${L1} 0.045 0.032"/></geometry>
      </collision>
      <xacro:box_inertial m="0.16" x="${L1}" y="0.045" z="0.032">
        <origin xyz="${L1/2} 0 0"/>
      </xacro:box_inertial>
    </link>
    <joint name="${side}_j2b" type="revolute">
      <parent link="${side}_upper_yoke_link"/>
      <child link="${side}_upper_arm_link"/>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0"/>
      <axis xyz="1 0 0"/>
      <limit lower="${-roll2_lim}" upper="${roll2_lim}" effort="12.0" velocity="3.0"/>
      <dynamics damping="0.7" friction="0.05"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- === J3 elbow + forearm === -->
    <!-- THE FOREARM IS 20 mm THICK, NOT 28, AND THAT IS A CLEARANCE DECISION.
         Its own hand folds back against it when the wrist bends past about 100 deg, which
         is the whole of the descent after the flip - waypoints 20 to 36, hand dropping
         from z=0.327 to 0.210. At 28 mm that contact was -2.0 mm, i.e. touching, on 17 of
         37 waypoints even with the elbow swivel free to route around it, because the
         swivel cannot move the forearm out of the path of the hand mounted on its end.
         20 mm makes it +2.0 mm clear everywhere. Verified stable at 3, 5 and 7 capsules
         per row (+2.8 to +2.9 mm with the finger also thinned), so the figure is real to
         about 0.2 mm rather than an artefact of the decomposition.
         Moving the hand further out instead (LG 0.070 -> 0.080) makes it WORSE, -0.5 mm:
         it swings the palm through a wider arc about the same wrist. -->
    <link name="${side}_forearm_link">
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="${PI/2} 0 0"/>
        <geometry><cylinder radius="0.020" length="0.048"/></geometry>
        <material name="dark_grey"/>
      </visual>
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="${L2/2} 0 0"/>
        <geometry><box size="${L2} 0.038 0.020"/></geometry>
        <material name="arm_blue"/>
      </visual>
      <collision>
        <origin xyz="${L2/2} 0 0"/>
        <geometry><box size="${L2} 0.038 0.020"/></geometry>
      </collision>
      <xacro:box_inertial m="0.13" x="${L2}" y="0.038" z="0.020">
        <origin xyz="${L2/2} 0 0"/>
      </xacro:box_inertial>
    </link>
    <joint name="${side}_j3" type="revolute">
      <parent link="${side}_upper_arm_link"/>
      <child link="${side}_forearm_link"/>
      <origin xyz="${L1} 0 0"/>
      <axis xyz="0 1 0"/>
      <limit lower="${-pitch3_lim}" upper="${pitch3_lim}" effort="12.0" velocity="3.0"/>
      <dynamics damping="0.7" friction="0.05"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- === SPHERICAL WRIST: J4 roll, J5 pitch, J6 roll ===
         All three origins are coincident at the wrist centre, so the three
         axes intersect. That is what decouples position from orientation. -->
    <link name="${side}_wrist_roll_link">
      <visual>
        <geometry><cylinder radius="0.018" length="0.030"/></geometry>
        <origin rpy="0 ${PI/2} 0"/>
        <material name="dark_grey"/>
      </visual>
      <collision>
        <geometry><cylinder radius="0.018" length="0.030"/></geometry>
        <origin rpy="0 ${PI/2} 0"/>
      </collision>
      <inertial>
        <mass value="0.07"/>
        <inertia ixx="1.5e-5" iyy="1.5e-5" izz="1.5e-5" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyz="0"/>
      </inertial>
    </link>
    <joint name="${side}_j4" type="revolute">
      <parent link="${side}_forearm_link"/>
      <child link="${side}_wrist_roll_link"/>
      <origin xyz="${L2} 0 0"/>
      <axis xyz="1 0 0"/>
      <limit lower="${-wroll_lim}" upper="${wroll_lim}" effort="12.0" velocity="3.0"/>
      <dynamics damping="0.5" friction="0.05"/>
    </joint>

    <link name="${side}_wrist_pitch_link">
      <visual>
        <geometry><sphere radius="0.019"/></geometry>
        <material name="dark_grey"/>
      </visual>
      <collision><geometry><sphere radius="0.019"/></geometry></collision>
      <inertial>
        <mass value="0.07"/>
        <inertia ixx="1.5e-5" iyy="1.5e-5" izz="1.5e-5" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyz="0"/>
      </inertial>
    </link>
    <joint name="${side}_j5" type="revolute">
      <parent link="${side}_wrist_roll_link"/>
      <child link="${side}_wrist_pitch_link"/>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0"/>
      <axis xyz="0 1 0"/>
      <limit lower="${-wpitch_lim}" upper="${wpitch_lim}" effort="12.0" velocity="3.0"/>
      <dynamics damping="0.5" friction="0.05"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- === J6 tool roll + palm === -->
    <link name="${side}_palm_link">
      <visual>
        <origin xyz="0.016 0 0"/>
        <geometry><cylinder radius="0.017" length="0.032"/></geometry>
        <material name="dark_grey"/>
      </visual>
      <visual>
                  <origin xyz="0.032 0 0"/>
        <geometry><box size="0.022 0.140 0.040"/></geometry>
        <material name="arm_blue"/>
      </visual>
      <collision>
        <origin xyz="0.032 0 0"/>
        <geometry><box size="0.022 0.140 0.040"/></geometry>
      </collision>
      <xacro:box_inertial m="0.11" x="0.022" y="0.140" z="0.040">
        <origin xyz="0.032 0 0"/>
      </xacro:box_inertial>
    </link>
    <joint name="${side}_j6" type="revolute">
      <parent link="${side}_wrist_pitch_link"/>
      <child link="${side}_palm_link"/>
      <origin xyz="0 0 0"/>
      <axis xyz="1 0 0"/>
      <limit lower="${-wroll_lim}" upper="${wroll_lim}" effort="12.0" velocity="3.0"/>
      <dynamics damping="0.5" friction="0.05"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- WRIST FORCE/TORQUE, as a UR5e has built in and a Panda estimates. Mounted on
         j6, so it measures the entire wrench transmitted to everything distal: palm,
         fingers, payload and any contact force. A position controller cannot feel that
         it is pressing on something; this can. Used by check_wrist_load and by the
         protective stop in send_pair. -->
    <gazebo reference="${side}_j6">
      <sensor name="${side}_wrist_ft" type="force_torque">
        <always_on>true</always_on>
        <update_rate>100</update_rate>
        <topic>/ft/${side}</topic>
        <force_torque>
          <frame>child</frame>
          <measure_direction>parent_to_child</measure_direction>
        </force_torque>
      </sensor>
    </gazebo>

    <!-- === fingers: prismatic along the palm's y === -->
    <xacro:macro name="finger" params="side fprefix faxis finger_effort">
      <link name="${side}_finger_${fprefix}_link">
        <!-- FINGER LENGTH: 0.080 m, starting 0.030 m out, so the tips reach 0.040 m
             PAST the grasp point. This number has now been wrong in both directions
             and the history is worth keeping.

             TOO LONG (0.075 m starting 0.044 m out, tips 0.049 m past the grasp
             point). With the grasp aimed at a floor-lying object's centre the tips
             ended up 0.009-0.019 m BELOW the ground plane, so the arms fought the
             floor on every grasp - which read as failure to converge and as a jerk
             at pickup.

             TOO SHORT (0.055 m, tips 0.015 m past the grasp point). The grasp point
             also had to move UP by grip_dz = 0.025 m to keep the palm out of the top
             of a 0.090 m object, and the two together left the fingertips 0.010 m
             ABOVE the object's axis. So the fingers never reached the widest part of
             the body: every contact was on the upper curve, which wedges a cylinder
             DOWNWARD out of the grip (about 5 N) and left only a 1.27x friction
             margin holding it. The user spotted this from the simulation before the
             arithmetic did.

             NOW. With the grasp 0.025 m above the axis and the tips 0.040 m past the
             grasp point, the fingers span z = 0.030 to 0.110 about a body whose axis
             sits at 0.045 and whose surface ends at 0.090. They STRADDLE the widest
             point, so contact happens where the surface normal is horizontal: no
             downward wedge, the grasp is self-centring, and the friction margin rises
             to about 2.7x. Floor clearance is still 0.030 m, well clear of the failure
             above. LG is unchanged, so the kinematics, the reach and the flip proof
             all stand. Validated by collision.py, which models the fingers as bodies.

             CONSEQUENCE: the binding half-width becomes the object's full radius
             rather than its width at the grasp height, so `close` is 0.048 for every
             object of this diameter - see E21 in ERROR_CATALOGUE.txt. -->

        <visual>
          <origin xyz="0.040 0 0"/>
          <geometry><box size="0.080 0.012 0.040"/></geometry>
          <material name="black"/>
        </visual>
        <!-- THE SUCTION CUP, flush with the pad face (see the note in the gazebo block).
             A URDF <visual> here, not inside <gazebo reference>: written there in URDF syntax
             Gazebo could not parse it ("origin ... not defined in SDF", "cylinder radius not
             defined") and silently dropped it, so the cups never existed in the simulation.
             rpy puts the disc's axis along the finger-travel axis, normal to the pad - the
             earlier 0 pi/2 0 turned it along the finger's length, 15 mm proud of the face.
             Visual only: flush with the pad, a collision shape would add nothing. -->
        <visual>
          <origin xyz="${RIDGE_X_CUP} ${-faxis * 0.004} 0" rpy="${PI/2} 0 0"/>
          <geometry><cylinder radius="0.015" length="0.004"/></geometry>
          <material name="black"/>
        </visual>
        <collision>
          <origin xyz="0.040 0 0"/>
          <geometry><box size="0.080 0.012 0.040"/></geometry>
        </collision>
        <inertial>
          <mass value="0.03"/>
          <inertia ixx="1.6e-5" iyy="8e-6" izz="8e-6" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyz="0"/>
        </inertial>
      </link>
      <!-- ==================== DISTAL PHALANX ====================
           THE RIGID PLATE CANNOT WRAP. The object's equator sits grip_dz + LG = 95 mm
           down the finger and the plate stops at 110 mm, so it reaches 15 mm past the
           widest point and then runs out. Everything tried on the flat plate - ridges,
           pad length, friction, force - only rearranged contact ABOVE or AT the equator,
           which is why a pinch on a 90 mm cylinder kept failing: it had 3 mm of lateral
           capture and no way to get underneath.
           A hinged second segment curls INWARD as the hand closes, so the fingertips
           reach BELOW the equator without extending the rigid plate into the floor - the
           constraint that capped every previous attempt (13.8 mm of floor clearance).
           Two of these per hand close into a basket rather than a pinch: the object is
           encaged instead of squeezed, so holding it stops depending on millimetre
           alignment. This is the two-phalanx underactuated finger used by real adaptive
           grippers (Robotiq 2F-85, Festo DHAS).
           IN HARDWARE one tendon and a return spring would drive both segments and the
           curl would be passive. Here the curl is COMMANDED in step with the proximal
           closure, because passive spring joints are not dependable across Gazebo physics
           engines. The coupling lives in grippers() in task_node.py. -->
      <link name="${side}_finger_${fprefix}_tip_link">
        <visual>
          <origin xyz="0.011 0 0"/>
          <geometry><box size="0.022 0.012 0.040"/></geometry>
          <material name="black"/>
        </visual>
        <collision>
          <origin xyz="0.011 0 0"/>
          <geometry><box size="0.022 0.012 0.040"/></geometry>
        </collision>
        <inertial>
          <mass value="0.012"/>
          <inertia ixx="4e-6" iyy="2e-6" izz="2e-6" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyz="0"/>
        </inertial>
      </link>
      <joint name="${side}_finger_${fprefix}_tip_joint" type="revolute">
        <parent link="${side}_finger_${fprefix}_link"/>
        <child link="${side}_finger_${fprefix}_tip_link"/>
        <!-- hinged at the far end of the proximal plate -->
        <origin xyz="0.080 0 0"/>
        <!-- about the object's axis, signed so POSITIVE always curls inward -->
        <axis xyz="0 0 ${-faxis}"/>
        <limit lower="0.0" upper="0.70" effort="${finger_effort}" velocity="1.5"/>
        <dynamics damping="0.3" friction="0.05"/>
      </joint>
      <gazebo reference="${side}_finger_${fprefix}_tip_link">
        <mu1>2.2</mu1><mu2>2.2</mu2>
        <!-- CONTACT SENSOR ON THE CURLED TIP. On a round object the distal phalanx curls in
             and the TIP bears on the body while the proximal pad is held a few mm off it
             (the finger still stalls +4 mm). The pad sensor below therefore read 0/2 on a
             hand that was gripping. The tip is part of the hand: sense it too. -->
        <sensor name="${side}_finger_${fprefix}_tip_contact" type="contact">
          <always_on>true</always_on>
          <update_rate>50</update_rate>
          <contact>
            <collision>${side}_finger_${fprefix}_tip_link_collision</collision>
          </contact>
          <topic>/contact/${side}_finger_${fprefix}_tip</topic>
        </sensor>
      </gazebo>

      <joint name="${side}_finger_${fprefix}_joint" type="prismatic">
        <parent link="${side}_palm_link"/>
        <child link="${side}_finger_${fprefix}_link"/>
        <origin xyz="0.030 0 0"/>
        <axis xyz="0 ${faxis} 0"/>
        <!-- EFFORT IS WHAT LIFTS THE ROBOT. Cut from 30 N to 8 N and now to 3 N.
             The fingers are commanded PAST the object's surface, so they stall
             against it and hold at whatever the effort limit allows. At 8 N per
             finger that is 32 N across four fingers, applied 0.30 m out in front of
             the wheel axle - enough of a moment to tip the base up on its casters.
             It showed up exactly when the grippers closed on an object and again when
             they released onto the shelf, which are the only two moments the fingers
             push on anything. 3 N still holds a 0.5 kg object at mu=1.5 with a factor
             of two, and cannot lift the robot. -->
        <!-- 6 N, was 3. Justified by measurement, not by the 0.375 N.m figure that
             originally motivated it (see the jaw comment - that was the horizontal case
             and does not apply). The worst single-handed requirement is 0.110 N.m, when
             the object is upright. With the ridges' 15 mm arm, 3 N gives 0.135 N.m =
             1.23x, under the 1.5x minimum; 6 N gives 0.270 N.m = 2.4x. Hence 6 N.
             30 N and 8 N were both rejected years of debugging ago for crushing objects
             and levering the base off its wheels - but that was a flat plate pressing
             into the body. This is two 8 mm ridges, a different contact. The base tilt
             check and the 30 N protective stop are the guards if it turns out worse. -->
        <!-- ASYMMETRIC BY DESIGN, and this is the load-bearing idea of the whole
                 section. Two RIGID grasps on one RIGID object is an over-constrained
                 closed chain: the arms cannot both reach their targets if they lag by
                 different amounts, and because position control has no give, the
                 mismatch becomes force. It was measured at 19.5 N on a wrist holding a
                 4.41 N object - 4.4x - and that is what sheared the object out.
                 Making the SUPPORT hand weak turns the chain into a friction clutch. It
                 can transmit at most mu * N of shear before it slides along the object,
                 so the internal force is CAPPED BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by how well
                 the controller tracks:
                     left  (grips)   2 x 6.0 N x mu 2.2 = 26.4 N  - holds the object
                     right (supports) 2 x 1.5 N x mu 2.2 =  6.6 N  - slips before it fights
                 Sliding along the object is not a failure here; it is the release valve.
                 Slip is measured against the LEFT hand, which is the one that holds. -->
            <limit lower="0.008" upper="${finger_travel}" effort="${finger_effort}" velocity="0.10"/>
        <dynamics damping="0.5" friction="0.1"/>
      </joint>
      <gazebo reference="${side}_finger_${fprefix}_link">
        <!-- SILICONE, Shore A 30. Real pad coefficients: nitrile ~0.9, polyurethane
               1.0-1.5, silicone 1.5-2.5. HONEST LIMITATION: the real advantage of
               silicone is that it CONFORMS, turning a line contact into an area contact,
               and a rigid-body simulator cannot represent that. 2.2 is standing in for an
               effect this model does not actually have. -->
        <!-- PAD LENGTH ALONG THE OBJECT'S AXIS: 0.060, was 0.030. THIS IS WHAT RESISTS
             GRAVITY TIPPING THE FAR END once only one hand is holding. A pad pressing
             with total force N over a patch of half-length a can resist a moment up to
             N*a before the pressure shifts to one edge and it rocks, so the resisting
             torque is set by the pad's LENGTH, not by the friction coefficient and not by
             any ridge. Two fingers at 6 N over a 60 mm pad give 12 x 0.030 = 0.36 N.m.
             The single-handed phase runs from the 70 deg handover to upright, where the
             requirement peaks at 0.110 N.m -> 3.3x.
             KNOWN BOUND, stated rather than buried: if the support hand were to let go
             completely while the object is still HORIZONTAL, the requirement would be
             0.375 N.m and this pad gives 0.96x. That case is not part of the sequence -
             both hands are on it until 70 deg - and the support hand has 3x margin on the
             load it carries. But it is the weakest point in the design.
             Lengthening the pad is free in every other respect: it grows ALONG the
             object, so it does not widen the hand, does not change floor clearance, and
             the two pads still finish 110 mm apart. It leaves 5 mm of object beyond the
             pad at END_CLEAR = 0.035. -->
        <!-- SUCTION CUP, one per finger, FLUSH WITH THE PAD FACE.
             WHY SUCTION. Every problem this gripper has had traces to one of two things:
             capture range (a pinch overlaps a 45 mm radius by 3 mm, against 20-60 mm of
             positioning error) or resistance to rotation (a flat pad on a cylinder is a
             LINE contact, so it resists a moment only over the pad's own width). Suction
             removes both, because it does not depend on geometry at all: the cup seals
             wherever it lands within a few mm of normal, and it holds over an AREA.
                 30 mm bellows cup, -60 kPa:  F = P.A = 60000 x pi(0.015)^2 = 42 N
                 against a 4.41 N object                                  -> 9.6x
             Bellows cups seal on curved surfaces routinely and a 45 mm radius is gentle.
             FLUSH, NOT PROUD, and that is deliberate. A cup standing proud of the pad
             would hold the flat face off the object - which is exactly the fault the
             curled tips had, where over-reaching flaps levered the bottle clear of the
             pads and left it balanced on two lines. Flush means the pad, the cup and the
             curled tip all bear at once, and `close` does not have to change. -->
        <!-- (the cup geometry is declared on the link itself, above) -->
          <!-- FLAT SILICONE PAD. The ridged jaw that used to be here was removed, and
               the reason is worth recording because it cost several rounds of work. It
               existed to give ONE hand enough torque to stop the object turning in the
               grip while that hand carried it 85 mm off centre. THAT LOAD CASE NO LONGER
               EXISTS: the left hand now grips the object at its CENTRE, so the gravity
               lever arm is the grip offset alone and the requirement falls by more than
               an order of magnitude. Geometry earns its complexity only if something
               needs it, and nothing does any more.
               Removing it also returns two things: the finger bodies stop sitting 6 mm
               wider, which is what consumed the entire radial barrier clearance in
               section A, and this file becomes shareable with the other sections again.
               What holds the object now is friction on a conforming pad - two fingers at
               6 N with mu 2.2 give 26.4 N of friction against a 4.41 N object. -->
          <mu1>2.2</mu1><mu2>2.2</mu2>
        <!-- CONTACT SENSOR, one per finger. The grasp check used to infer contact from
             how far short the finger stalled against its commanded position. That is
             indirect: it cannot distinguish the object from anything else the finger
             happens to touch, and it silently depends on the commanded over-travel
             being larger than the detection threshold. This reports contact as a fact.
             Four of them, so "one hand gripping and the other closing on air" - the
             failure that kept recurring - is visible per finger rather than averaged
             away. -->
        <sensor name="${side}_finger_${fprefix}_contact" type="contact">
          <always_on>true</always_on>
          <update_rate>50</update_rate>
          <contact>
            <collision>${side}_finger_${fprefix}_link_collision</collision>
          </contact>
          <topic>/contact/${side}_finger_${fprefix}</topic>
        </sensor>
      </gazebo>
    </xacro:macro>
    <xacro:finger side="${side}" fprefix="l" faxis="1" finger_effort="${finger_effort}"/>
    <xacro:finger side="${side}" fprefix="r" faxis="-1" finger_effort="${finger_effort}"/>

  </xacro:macro>
</robot>
